T:Cân y Cathreinwr
T:Cân y Cathreiniwr
T:The Ox-Driver's Song
R:cân
S:https://amgueddfa.cymru/casgliadau/caneuongwerin/?id=1 (collected by D. Roy Saer)
N:Sung by Robert Thomas (born 1865), a farmer, of Rock House, Twynyrodyn, near Wenvoe, Glamorgan; recorded September 1953 (St Fagans tape 4). At 13 he had sung such stanzas while helping to drive the ploughing oxen at Doghill Farm, Dyffryn, near St Nicholas, around 1878. Glamorgan was the last part of Wales to plough with oxen (until about 1880), and the drivers sang stanzas like these, mostly in the triban metre, each ending with the call to the oxen. The words are in the Vale of Glamorgan dialect, with the letters the singer dropped in brackets. Verses 1 and 2 are nonsense, 3 to 5 are triads.
N:The grace note before "Gan" is a small note on the score. The held note on "lladd" (fermata) would presumably not have been held so long when the song was sung to the oxen's walking pace.
